#!/usr/bin/env bash
# assert-mutation-run — decide whether a finished Stryker run actually MEASURED what
# its config asked for. Called by .github/workflows/mutation.yml after every shard;
# runnable by hand over a saved log, which is the point (see "Verifying this" below).
#
# usage: scripts/assert-mutation-run <stryker-log> <stryker-config> <expected-mutants> <expected-tests> <shard-name> [canary]
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. A mutation gate has two ways to be green, and only one of them is
# honest. This repository has already shipped the dishonest one twice: once as a
# vacuous run (0 mutants, `project_mutation_gate`), and once as a run that quietly
# substituted a different test suite for the one the config named (#99 — Stryker's
# solution mode ignores `test-projects`, so the expressions shard ran 2211 tests
# instead of 722 and could not finish inside 120 minutes). Both reported success.
#
# The first version of the second guard was `grep -q 'will mutate solution'` — a
# NEGATIVE assertion, and therefore fail-OPEN: reword that log line in any Stryker
# release and the guard silently stops guarding, which is the same class of defect it
# was added to catch. Everything below is a POSITIVE assertion instead: it states the
# number the run must report, and a missing or reworded line fails the check.
#
# WHY NOT ASSERT THE MUTANT COUNT AGAINST SOLUTION MODE. Measured, because it is the
# obvious idea and it does not work: solution mode changes which TEST PROJECTS run,
# not which files are mutated. Same commit, `stryker-config.expressions.json`, both
# modes report "4064 mutants created" and "834 total mutants will be tested"; only
# "Number of tests found" moves (722 -> 2211). data-sqlite likewise: 38 and 38, with
# 403 -> 783 tests. So the assertion that catches solution mode is the TEST-PROJECT
# COUNT (check 2); the two counts are asserted for different failures (checks 3, 4).
#
# NO PIPELINES INTO grep. The log is normalised once into a temp file and every check
# greps that file. This is not style: `printf '%s\n' "$text" | grep -q PATTERN` under
# `set -o pipefail` yields 141, not 0, whenever grep matches early and exits while
# printf is still writing — which is what happens on any real (multi-hundred-KB) log,
# because "Analyzing N test project(s)." appears near the top. That inverted check 2,
# and would have failed every shard on the first real run while reporting that the log
# did not contain a line it had just quoted back. scripts/test-assert-mutation-run
# pins it with an oversized fixture; keep that case if you touch this.
#
# CHECK 5 IS A DIFFERENT KIND OF CHECK FROM 1-4, and it is worth saying why before
# reading it. Checks 1-4 all assert that the run MEASURED THE RIGHT THING — the right
# suite, a plausible mutant count, a suite that did not shrink. #142 was a run that
# measured exactly the right thing and LIED ABOUT THE RESULT, and it was two defects
# stacked: Stryker's MTP runner counts a test in `State: error` as a failing test —
# i.e. as a kill — and carries no message for it (upstream, unchanged in 5.0.0); and two
# of OUR tests error on the second and later runs inside one test-host process, which is
# exactly how Stryker runs a suite. One such test converts every mutant into a false
# kill, silently, so essentially every mutant comes back Killed regardless of the mutant.
# No count can reveal that, and neither can a percentage: the output is
# indistinguishable from a genuinely adversarial suite.
#
# Our half is FIXED as of 2026-09-14 (test/_shared/mutation/, gated on the env var
# Stryker sets in the test host): Verify's process-wide prefix guard and the public-API
# approval gate are the two, and #142 records how each one lied. The upstream half — an errored
# node counted as a kill, with its message dropped — is unchanged, which is precisely
# why this check stays: it is what catches the next thing that errors for a reason that
# is not the mutant.
#
# So check 5 asserts the one thing a lying runner cannot fake: a KNOWN-SURVIVING
# mutant must be reported as Survived. `stryker-config.canary.json` mutates
# src/MMLib.Alvo.Testing/Migrations/InMemoryDescriptorVersionStore.cs against
# test/MMLib.Alvo.Abstractions.Tests, a suite that mentions neither that class nor
# anything it touches (`grep -rn 'DescriptorVersion' test/MMLib.Alvo.Abstractions.Tests`
# → nothing), so EVERY one of its mutants must survive. When the 6th argument is the
# word `canary`, this script refuses any run that reports a kill.
#
# THE CHECK IS ONE-SIDED, AND THAT IS WHY IT CANNOT BE FLAKY IN THE DANGEROUS
# DIRECTION. A reported kill on the canary is always a lie, whatever caused it. A
# canary that comes back all-Survived might be luck rather than health — which is
# exactly the direction a guard is allowed to be generous in.
#
# MEASURED, and this is why the canary is worth its 17 seconds: 13 mutants, 142 tests,
# and before the fix the run reported **9 Killed / 4 Survived** — nine kills by a suite
# that cannot reach the code. That was #142, reproduced in a quarter of a minute instead
# of the eight it takes on its real repro. Identical on Stryker **4.16.0 and 5.0.0**
# (verified against Dependabot's #220), and WORSE at `--concurrency 1` (12 Killed /
# 1 Survived) — not because serialising is worse, but because one process then runs more
# mutants, and every run of a process after its first is the poisoned one. Raising
# `additional-timeout` to 120 s changed nothing either: nothing timed out.
# Since 2026-09-14 the same run reports **0 Killed / 13 Survived**, at both
# concurrencies, which is the honest answer by construction.
#
# VERIFYING THIS SCRIPT: scripts/test-assert-mutation-run runs it over real Stryker
# logs captured in both working directories. To try one by hand:
#   scripts/assert-mutation-run scripts/fixtures/mutation-logs/data-ef-solution-mode.txt \
#                               stryker-config.data-ef.json 596 858 data-ef
# It must fail check 2 on a solution-mode log and pass on a `test/`-scoped one. And for
# the canary, over a real run of it (17 s: cd test && dotnet tool run dotnet-stryker
# -f ../stryker-config.canary.json --concurrency 4):
#   scripts/assert-mutation-run <that log> stryker-config.canary.json 13 142 canary canary
# It must PASS. If it ever fails again, the kill count it names is the finding, and the
# check-5 failure message says what to do about it.
set -uo pipefail

log="${1:?usage: assert-mutation-run <log> <config> <expected-mutants> <expected-tests> <shard-name>}"
config="${2:?missing stryker config path}"
expected_mutants="${3:?missing expected mutant count}"
expected_tests="${4:?missing expected test count}"
shard="${5:?missing shard name}"
# Optional 6th argument, literally the word `canary`. Anything else is refused rather
# than ignored: a typo that silently turned check 5 off would leave the one check that
# can detect a lying runner quietly absent, which is the defect class it exists for.
canary="${6:-}"
case "$canary" in
    ''|canary) ;;
    *) echo "::error::Mutation run ($shard): the 6th argument must be the word 'canary' or absent, got '$canary'."; exit 1 ;;
esac

fail() {
    echo "::error::Mutation run ($shard): $1"
    exit 1
}

require_number() { # <value> <what it is>
    case "$1" in
        ''|*[!0-9]*)
            # Not pedantry: `$(( ))` on a non-numeric value trips `set -u`, and on
            # bash 3.2 (macOS, where the header invites running this by hand) the
            # script then exits 0 — a guard failing open on a typo in its own
            # arguments.
            fail "$2 must be a whole number, got '$1'." ;;
        0*)
            # `$(( 0834 ))` is OCTAL in bash, so a leading zero would either compute a
            # silently wrong floor (010 -> 8) or abort on a digit >= 8 ("value too great
            # for base"). A bare 0 is rejected by the same arm, which is right for a
            # different reason: a floor of zero is a check that cannot fail.
            fail "$2 must not have a leading zero (bash reads 0NNN as octal), got '$1'." ;;
    esac
}

# The same guard for a number READ OUT OF THE LOG rather than passed in as an argument,
# and it differs in exactly one case: a bare `0` is legitimate here. require_number
# rejects it deliberately — a declared floor of zero is a check that cannot fail — but
# `Timeout: 0` is the healthiest observation this script can make, so rejecting it would
# fail every honest run. Everything else is the same, and for the same reason:
# `$(( 08 ))` is an octal error, which under `set -uo pipefail` (no `-e`) aborts the
# enclosing compound command and lets the script run on to its success line.
require_log_number() { # <value> <what it is>
    case "$1" in
        ''|*[!0-9]*) fail "$2 must be a whole number, got '$1'." ;;
        0|[1-9]*)    ;;
        *)           fail "$2 must not have a leading zero (bash reads 0NNN as octal), got '$1'." ;;
    esac
}

require_number "$expected_mutants" "the expected mutant count"
require_number "$expected_tests" "the expected test count"

[ -f "$log" ] || fail "no Stryker log at '$log' — the run produced nothing to judge."
[ -f "$config" ] || fail "no Stryker config at '$config'."
command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || fail "jq is not installed, so the config's declared test-projects cannot be read. Refusing to pass a run this script was unable to check."

# Stryker's progress reporter writes \r-terminated lines; normalise once, to a FILE,
# so that no check has to pipe into grep (see the header).
#
# THE ANSI STRIP IS NOT COSMETIC, and it is here because it cost a red canary on the
# first run that ever exercised check 5 on CI. Stryker's summary block is written
# through Spectre.Console, which decides on colour by asking the environment rather
# than by asking whether stdout is a terminal. On a developer machine redirecting to a
# file it emits `Killed:    9`; on a GitHub runner — same command, also not a TTY — it
# emits `Killed:   \e[38;5;13m 0\e[0m`. Checks 1-4 never noticed because they read
# Stryker's `[HH:mm:ss INF]` lines, which are not colourised; check 5 is the only one
# that reads the summary, so it was the only one that could see this, and every
# committed fixture had been captured with `>` on macOS. The failure mode was the worst
# available: not a wrong verdict but NO verdict — "never reports 'Killed: N'" on a run
# whose numbers were sitting right there, correct.
#
# The `?` in the character class is not padding either: it covers PRIVATE-MODE CSI
# sequences such as `\e[?25h` (cursor show/hide), which a progress reporter emits and
# which `[0-9;]*` does not match. Without it such a sequence left on a summary line
# makes the `^Killed:` / `^Timeout:` anchors miss, and check 6 then skips SILENTLY —
# demonstrated, not hypothesised. No committed capture contains one today; that is the
# argument for adding the character now rather than after one does.
norm="$(mktemp)"
trap 'rm -f "$norm"' EXIT
tr '\r' '\n' < "$log" | command sed -E 's/\x1B\[[0-9;?]*[A-Za-z]//g' > "$norm"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Check 1 — vacuity. A run that discovered or tested zero mutants is not a passing
# run, it is a broken one (stryker-net#3094). Its failure mode differs from the
# checks below: this one catches a run that measured NOTHING, they catch a run that
# measured the WRONG thing.
#
# Note `[[:space:]]+`: Stryker column-pads the count, emitting
# "0     total mutants will be tested", so the single-space pattern this check
# inherited from the inline workflow step could never match and that whole
# alternative was dead.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
if command grep -qE '(^|[^0-9])0[[:space:]]+total mutants will be tested|unable to calculate a mutation score|Number of tests found: 0([^0-9]|$)' "$norm"; then
    fail "tested zero mutants (vacuous run) — see the log above. Failing the gate instead of a false green (stryker-net#3094)."
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Check 2 — the suite is the one the config named. Stryker prints "Analyzing N test
# project(s)." only when it resolves `test-projects` itself; in solution mode it
# prints "Stryker will mutate solution ..." and discovers test projects on its own,
# so the line is absent and this check fails. N comes from the config rather than a
# hand-maintained constant, so it cannot drift out of sync with what it guards.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
declared="$(jq -r '.["stryker-config"]["test-projects"] | if . == null then "null" else length end' "$config" 2>/dev/null)"
case "$declared" in
    ''|null|0)
        fail "config '$config' declares no test-projects. Stryker would fall back to the working directory, which is not a measurement anyone chose."
        ;;
esac

if ! command grep -qF "Analyzing $declared test project(s)." "$norm"; then
    reported="$(command grep -oE 'Analyzing [0-9]+ test project\(s\)\.' "$norm" | head -1)"
    tests_found="$(command grep -oE 'Number of tests found: [0-9]+' "$norm" | head -1)"
    fail "expected Stryker to analyse exactly $declared test project(s) (the number '$config' declares), but the log does not say so — it says '${reported:-<nothing>}' (${tests_found:-tests found: unknown}).
    The usual cause is Stryker entering SOLUTION mode, which discards test-projects and substitutes every test project referencing the mutated assembly: a silent ~3x suite inflation whose only other symptom is a timeout weeks later (#99). Run from 'test/', not the repository root.
    If a Stryker upgrade merely reworded the line, fix this assertion — do not delete it."
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Check 3 — the mutate glob still matches. Different failure from check 1: a glob that
# stops matching after a rename or a folder move does not report zero, it reports a
# COLLAPSED count, which the vacuity check waves through and a score cannot reveal
# (fewer mutants, same percentage). Anchored on the matrix's measured count.
#
# TOLERANCE: a one-sided floor at 60 % of the declared count, not a band.
#   - One-sided because only a collapse is a defect. More mutants than declared means
#     more code under measurement, which is never the bug — so growth must not fail a
#     run, or every ordinary PR that adds a line would need this file edited too.
#   - 60 % because the declared numbers are a dated snapshot, not a contract, and
#     ordinary drift between refreshes is single-digit percent. Losing two fifths of a
#     600-800 mutant shard is not drift; it is a glob that stopped matching or an area
#     that got excluded. The looseness is deliberate: check 2 is the exact one, and it
#     can afford to be exact because it is derived from the config.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
tested="$(command grep -oE '[0-9]+[[:space:]]+total mutants will be tested' "$norm" | command grep -oE '^[0-9]+' | head -1)"
[ -n "$tested" ] || fail "the log never reports 'N total mutants will be tested', so there is no verdict to trust. Either the run did not reach the mutation loop, or a Stryker upgrade reworded the line — in which case fix this assertion rather than deleting it."

mutant_floor=$(( expected_mutants * 60 / 100 ))
if [ "$tested" -lt "$mutant_floor" ]; then
    fail "tested $tested mutants, but '$config' measured $expected_mutants when this shard was last calibrated (floor $mutant_floor = 60 %).
    A collapse this size is not ordinary drift: check the mutate globs against the files that moved. If the drop is legitimate, update the shard's 'mutants:' in .github/workflows/mutation.yml so the floor tracks reality."
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Check 4 — the suite did not shrink. Check 2 catches the suite being SUBSTITUTED
# (solution mode inflates it); this catches it being GUTTED, which is the failure
# mutation testing exists to detect in the first place — "the adversarial suite
# stopped biting". Without it, deleting half of MMLib.Alvo.Tests passes checks 1-3
# unchanged (same projects, same mutants); before #142 was fixed the score could not be
# relied on to notice either, and check 5 is what keeps that from coming back. Same
# one-sided 60 % floor, same reasoning: tests are
# added far more often than removed, so only a collapse is a signal.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
found="$(command grep -oE 'Number of tests found: [0-9]+' "$norm" | command grep -oE '[0-9]+$' | head -1)"
[ -n "$found" ] || fail "the log never reports 'Number of tests found: N', so the size of the suite behind this verdict is unknown. If a Stryker upgrade reworded the line, fix this assertion rather than deleting it."

test_floor=$(( expected_tests * 60 / 100 ))
if [ "$found" -lt "$test_floor" ]; then
    fail "ran against $found tests, but this shard was calibrated at $expected_tests (floor $test_floor = 60 %).
    A suite that lost two fifths of its tests is the exact regression this gate exists to catch, and it is invisible to the mutation score. If the shrinkage is legitimate, update the shard's 'suite:' in .github/workflows/mutation.yml."
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Check 6 — the timeout share, i.e. how much of the score is not evidence.
#
# WHAT IT CATCHES, and it is a THIRD way for this gate to be green dishonestly, after
# the vacuous run and #142. Stryker's score is (Killed + Timeout) / tested — verified
# arithmetically against all seven shards of run 34828632562, not quoted from the
# docs, which do not state it. A Timeout is therefore in the NUMERATOR, while the
# report's own `killedBy` array for a timed-out mutant is EMPTY, exactly like a
# survivor's. Counting it as a detection is right when the mutant caused the hang (an
# endless loop is a real kill) and wrong when the run merely ran out of time.
#
# WHY IT IS USUALLY THE SECOND ONE HERE, measured. `timeout = (initialTestRunTime +
# coveringTestsTime) * timeout-ratio + additional-timeout`, and every config sets
# `coverage-analysis: off`, so each mutant re-runs the WHOLE assembly. The threshold is
# derived from the initial run, which happens ALONE; mutant runs happen four at a time
# (`--concurrency 4` on a 4-vCPU runner), so each is several times slower than the run
# the threshold came from. Bail-out then splits the population cleanly: a KILLED mutant
# aborts at the first failing test and finishes fast, a SURVIVING one runs to the end —
# so on a slow leg "survived" and "timed out" become the same event.
#
# THE CONTROLLED EXPERIMENT, on the identical 565 mutants of data-ef-rest's own mutate
# set, changing only the test-projects list and then only the timeout:
#
#   suite                                       Killed  Survived  Timeout  reported
#   EntityFrameworkCore.Tests alone                309       256        0    54.69 %
#   + Data.Sqlite.Tests                            391        31      143    94.51 %
#   + Data.Sqlite.Tests, additional-timeout 300 s  397       168        0    70.27 %
#
# Per-mutant, A -> B: 309 Killed->Killed, 82 Survived->Killed, 143 Survived->Timeout,
# and ZERO Killed->Timeout. Not one killed mutant ever timed out. Endless loops do not
# select for mutants that were about to survive; a threshold below the suite's own
# runtime does exactly that. Raising the timeout resolved all 143 — 137 to Survived,
# 6 to Killed — which is what settles it.
#
# WHAT IT COST, and this is why the check is worth a shard's failure: data-ef-core
# reported 99.82 % (293 Killed / 1 Survived / 249 Timeout) and measures 59.30 %
# (321 Killed / 221 Survived / 1 Timeout) once the timeout is raised. It had 221 real
# survivors and the gate saw one. data-sqlite reported 100.00 % on 22 Killed / 0
# Survived / 22 Timeout. THE TWO LEGS THAT PASSED WERE THE TWO WHOSE PASS WAS MADE OF
# TIMEOUTS.
#
# THE CEILING IS 20 %, ONE-SIDED, and here is the calibration it comes from — the same
# run, timeouts as a share of tested mutants:
#
#   lying:   data-ef-core 45.86 %, data-sqlite 50.00 %
#   honest:  data-ef-rest 0 %, data-postgresql 0 %, rules/auth/rest 2.10 %,
#            expressions 7.56 %
#
# 20 % sits with a factor of ~2.3 of margin on both sides. It is deliberately NOT 10 %:
# expressions' 7.56 % has not been re-measured under a raised timeout, and an endless
# loop is genuinely plausible in an expression evaluator, so some of those 68 may be
# real detections. Tighten this once expressions is measured; do not tighten it on the
# strength of this comment alone.
#
# ONE-SIDED for the same reason as checks 3 and 4: a run with FEWER timeouts than
# calibrated is never the defect. And the fix when it fires is `additional-timeout` in
# the shard's config plus the `timeout:` budget that buys — NOT deleting the check and
# not lowering `break`, both of which rebuild the lying gate by a slower route.
#
# Skipped for the canary, which asserts something strictly stronger below: every mutant
# must be Survived, so a single timeout there already fails check 5 with a better
# diagnosis than this one could give.
#
# WHY IT IS CONDITIONAL ON THE SUMMARY BLOCK, and why that is not the fail-open shape
# this file keeps repairing. Checks 1-4 read Stryker's `[HH:mm:ss INF]` lines, which a
# run prints before the mutation loop; this check reads the SUMMARY, which only a
# COMPLETED run prints. The repository's own discovery probes — started, watched until
# the mutant list appears, then killed by hand — are legitimate logs with no summary at
# all, and four committed fixtures are exactly that, said so in their own headers. So
# the condition is "did this run finish", read as "is there a `Killed:` line", and it is
# fail-closed where it matters: once the summary is there, a missing `Timeout:` line is
# a rewording and is REFUSED rather than read as zero.
#
# The residual gap is stated rather than waved away: a Stryker release that dropped the
# whole summary block would skip this check silently. What covers that is the canary,
# which requires `Killed:` and `Survived:` positively and runs FIRST in the matrix, so
# it fails before any score-bearing leg is believed — the reason it is first.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
if [ "$canary" != canary ] && command grep -qE '^Killed:[[:space:]]+[0-9]+' "$norm"; then
    # `tail -1`, NOT `head -1`, and the difference is the difference between a guard and
    # a suggestion. The summary block is the LAST thing a completed run prints, so the
    # last match is the real one; with `head -1` a single earlier line reading
    # "Timeout:  0" anywhere above it wins and the check passes on a 45 %-timeout log.
    # Demonstrated, so it is a fact rather than a worry: inserting one such line at the
    # top of scripts/fixtures/mutation-logs/data-ef-core-timeout-inflated.txt turned this
    # check green, and the case below pins it. (Check 5 still reads `head -1` for
    # Killed:/Survived:. That is pre-existing and has its own cases; it is not changed
    # here because a silent behaviour change under an unrelated fix is how the last three
    # defects in this file got in.)
    timed_out="$(command grep -oE '^Timeout:[[:space:]]+[0-9]+' "$norm" | command grep -oE '[0-9]+$' | tail -1)"
    [ -n "$timed_out" ] || fail "the log reports 'Killed: N' but never 'Timeout: N', so the share of this score that is a timeout rather than a kill is unknown. Stryker prints both in the same summary block; if an upgrade reworded it, fix this assertion rather than deleting it."

    # BOTH operands go through require_number before any $(( )). This is not belt and
    # braces: $tested and $timed_out are read out of a LOG, and require_number's own
    # comment above says exactly what happens without it — `$(( 08 ))` is an octal error,
    # the arithmetic aborts the enclosing `if`, and the script runs on to its success line
    # and exits 0. A guard failing open on a malformed input is this file's recurring
    # defect, and check 3's floor does not help: it validates MAGNITUDE, not format.
    require_log_number "$tested" "the tested-mutant count read from the log"
    require_log_number "$timed_out" "the timeout count read from the log"

    timeout_ceiling=$(( tested * 20 / 100 ))
    if [ "$timed_out" -gt "$timeout_ceiling" ]; then
        timeout_pct=$(( timed_out * 100 / tested ))
        fail "$timed_out of $tested mutants TIMED OUT (${timeout_pct} %, ceiling ${timeout_ceiling} = 20 % of $tested, rounded down).
    Stryker scores a timeout as a kill — the score is (Killed + Timeout) / tested — but a timed-out mutant
    names no killing test (its 'killedBy' is empty, exactly like a survivor's). At this share the reported
    score is mostly not evidence: measured on this repository, data-ef-core reported 99.82 % with 249 of 543
    timing out and measures 59.30 % once the timeout is raised, with 221 real survivors the gate had recorded
    as one.
    THE CAUSE IS ALMOST NEVER ENDLESS LOOPS. With 'coverage-analysis: off' every mutant re-runs the whole
    assembly, and the timeout threshold is derived from an initial test run that happened ALONE while mutant
    runs happen four at a time. Bail-out makes killed mutants fast and surviving ones slow, so on a slow leg
    surviving and timing out become the same event. The tell is the split: if timeouts were real hangs they
    would land on killed and surviving mutants alike.
    THE FIX is 'additional-timeout' in '$config' — raise it above the suite's own wall clock under
    concurrency — together with this shard's 'timeout:' budget in .github/workflows/mutation.yml, because a
    survivor that runs to completion costs more than one that was cut off (measured: ~2x on the same mutant
    set). Do NOT lower 'break', and do not delete this check: both rebuild a gate that reports a number
    nothing measured."
    fi
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Check 5 — the canary. Only for a run whose config is a known-uncovered pairing (see
# the header). Reads the summary block Stryker prints unconditionally:
#
#   Killed:    9
#   Survived:  4
#
# Both lines are required, positively: a missing "Killed:" line must fail rather than
# read as zero kills, which is the fail-OPEN shape every other check here was already
# repaired for once.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
if [ "$canary" = canary ]; then
    killed="$(command grep -oE '^Killed:[[:space:]]+[0-9]+' "$norm" | command grep -oE '[0-9]+$' | head -1)"
    survived="$(command grep -oE '^Survived:[[:space:]]+[0-9]+' "$norm" | command grep -oE '[0-9]+$' | head -1)"

    [ -n "$killed" ] || fail "the canary run's log never reports 'Killed: N', so there is no verdict to judge. If a Stryker upgrade reworded the summary, fix this assertion rather than deleting it."
    [ -n "$survived" ] || fail "the canary run's log never reports 'Survived: N'. Same rule as above: fix the assertion, do not drop the check."

    if [ "$killed" -ne 0 ]; then
        fail "the CANARY reported $killed killed mutant(s) (and $survived survived).
    Every mutant in this config is known-surviving by construction — '$config' mutates a file that its
    declared test project does not reference at all — so a kill here is the runner reporting a verdict it
    cannot have reached. The mechanism is #142's: the MTP runner counts a test in 'State: error' as a failing
    test and drops its message, and Stryker re-runs the whole suite inside ONE test-host process, once per
    mutant. Any test that is only correct on a process's FIRST run therefore errors on every later mutant,
    and each such error is counted as a kill. It is not flakiness and not load: expect it to be exactly one
    test, on every mutant but the first.
    FIRST THING TO CHECK: whether Stryker still sets STRYKER_MUTANT_FILE (mtp) or STRYKER_MUTANT_ID_CONTROL_VAR
    (VsTest) in the test host. test/_shared/mutation/MutationRun.cs keys the fix off those two names, and an
    upstream rename turns it off silently. Then get the dropped message — Stryker never carries it, but MTP
    does: re-run with TESTINGPLATFORM_DIAGNOSTIC=1, TESTINGPLATFORM_DIAGNOSTIC_VERBOSITY=trace and
    TESTINGPLATFORM_DIAGNOSTIC_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY=<dir>, then grep the .diag files for
    ErrorTestNodeStateProperty. Do not read Stryker's own trace to name the test: it prints one node's state
    next to the FOLLOWING node's name, which is what sent #222 after three innocent tests.
    Nothing this gate says about any other shard's score is evidence while this check is red."
    fi

    if [ "$survived" -ne "$tested" ]; then
        fail "the canary tested $tested mutants but reports only $survived survived (and $killed killed).
    Every one must survive. A mutant that is neither killed nor survived (timeout, error, no coverage) is
    also a verdict this pairing cannot produce honestly."
    fi

    echo "[assert-mutation-run] $shard OK — CANARY honest: $tested mutants, all $survived survived, 0 killed."
    exit 0
fi

echo "[assert-mutation-run] $shard OK — $declared test project(s) as configured, $tested mutants tested (declared $expected_mutants, floor $mutant_floor), $found tests (declared $expected_tests, floor $test_floor)."
